<h1>Hostel Desk — Technical Setup Guide</h1>

<p>Below is the structure and the technical setup required for the <strong>Hostel Desk &mdash; PHP + MySQL Backend</strong> portal. This document is designed for system administrators and developers to ensure a smooth deployment process.</p>

<div class="code-block">
hostel-portal/
├── database/
│   └── schema.sql          ← import this first
├── backend/
│   ├── config.php          ← DB credentials
│   ├── db.php               ← PDO connection
│   ├── helpers.php          ← shared helpers (json, sessions, ticket numbers)
│   ├── setup_warden.php     ← CLI script to create a warden login
│   └── api/
│       ├── submit_complaint.php   POST  — student files a ticket
│       ├── list_complaints.php    GET   — student lookup / warden dashboard
│       ├── update_status.php      POST  — warden updates a ticket (auth required)
│       ├── warden_login.php       POST  — warden signs in
│       ├── warden_logout.php      POST  — warden signs out
│       └── session_check.php      GET   — is a warden already logged in?
└── frontend/
    └── index.html           ← the portal UI (calls the API above)
</div>

<h2>1. Create the Database</h2>
<p>Run the following command to import the database schema:</p>
<div class="code-block">
mysql -u root -p < database/schema.sql
</div>
<p>This creates the <span class="inline-code">hostel_complaint_portal</span> database with three tables: <span class="inline-code">wardens</span>, <span class="inline-code">complaints</span>, and <span class="inline-code">ticket_counters</span>. The counters are used to generate gap-free <span class="inline-code">HC-2026-0001</span> style ticket numbers safely under concurrent submissions.</p>
<p><em>Note: No warden account is seeded in the SQL file on purpose — passwords should be hashed securely by PHP's <span class="inline-code">password_hash()</span>, rather than being pasted into a <span class="inline-code">.sql</span> file.</em></p>

<h2>2. Configure the Database Connection</h2>
<p>You can edit <span class="inline-code">backend/config.php</span> directly, or set the following environment variables on your server: <span class="inline-code">DB_HOST</span>, <span class="inline-code">DB_PORT</span>, <span class="inline-code">DB_NAME</span>, <span class="inline-code">DB_USER</span>, and <span class="inline-code">DB_PASS</span>.</p>

<h2>3. Create Your First Warden Login</h2>
<p>Run the following command in the terminal to set up your first warden:</p>
<div class="code-block">
cd backend
php setup_warden.php warden1 "a-strong-password" "Mr. R. Kulkarni"
</div>
<p>Run this script again with the same username any time you want to reset that specific warden's password.</p>

<h2>4. Run the Application</h2>
<p>For local testing with PHP's built-in server, execute the following from the root <span class="inline-code">hostel-portal/</span> folder:</p>
<div class="code-block">
php -S localhost:8000
</div>
<p>Then navigate your browser to: <strong>http://localhost:8000/frontend/index.html</strong>.</p>
<p>For a production deployment, upload the entire <span class="inline-code">hostel-portal/</span> folder to any PHP 8+ / MySQL host (e.g., shared hosting, VPS) and point your browser to <span class="inline-code">frontend/index.html</span>. The frontend calls the API using a relative path (<span class="inline-code">../backend/api/...</span>). Keeping the <span class="inline-code">frontend/</span> and <span class="inline-code">backend/</span> folders as siblings is all that is required — no extra configuration needed.</p>
<p>If you ever deploy the frontend on a <strong>different domain</strong> than the backend, you will need to add CORS headers to each <span class="inline-code">api/*.php</span> file and update the cookie <span class="inline-code">samesite</span> handling in <span class="inline-code">helpers.php</span>. The default same-origin setup does not require these modifications.</p>

<h2>5. How Login and Security Works</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Students:</strong> Do not have accounts. They identify a ticket by name + room number. <span class="inline-code">list_complaints.php?mode=student&amp;lookup=...</span> only ever returns tickets matching that specific search query.</li>
    <li><strong>Wardens:</strong> Log in with a username/password (<span class="inline-code">warden_login.php</span>), which establishes a PHP session (via an <span class="inline-code">httponly</span> cookie). Both <span class="inline-code">update_status.php</span> and the warden dashboard reject requests with a <span class="inline-code">401 Unauthorized</span> error if no warden is logged in.</li>
    <li><strong>Password Security:</strong> Passwords are hashed with bcrypt via <span class="inline-code">password_hash()</span> and verified using <span class="inline-code">password_verify()</span>. They are never stored or compared in plain text.</li>
    <li><strong>Database Security:</strong> All queries use PDO prepared statements to ensure user input is safely handled and never concatenated into SQL strings.</li>
</ul>

